---
// Two radios on the same LoRa channel settings but different native mesh protocols.
type Row = { label: string; note: string; kind?: 'ok' | 'bad' | 'unknown' };
const rows: Row[] = [
  { label: 'App', note: 'separate apps' },
  { label: 'Phone–radio link', note: 'local only' },
  { label: 'Mesh protocol', note: 'different format', kind: 'bad' },
  { label: 'Framing', note: 'not shown to match', kind: 'unknown' },
  { label: 'Channel settings', note: 'same', kind: 'ok' },
];
const y = (i: number) => 14 + i * 58;
---

<figure class="diagram not-content">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 360 310" role="img" aria-labelledby="ls-title ls-desc">
    <title id="ls-title">Two radios: same channel settings, framing not shown to match, different mesh protocols</title>
    <desc id="ls-desc">
      Left stack: a Meshtastic node. Right stack: a MeshCore node. The bottom layer, channel settings
      (frequency, bandwidth, spreading factor, coding rate), matches, so each radio's signal lands on the
      other's channel. The framing layer, which includes the sync word, is set in different code by the two
      projects and is not shown to match, so receiving a whole frame is not established. The mesh protocol
      layer differs, so even a received frame could not be read as a native message. The app and phone link
      layers are separate on each side.
    </desc>
    <text x="64" y="10" class="hdr" text-anchor="middle">Meshtastic node</text>
    <text x="296" y="10" class="hdr" text-anchor="middle">MeshCore node</text>
    {rows.map((r, i) => (
      <g>
        <rect x="4" y={y(i) + 4} width="120" height="44" rx="3" class:list={['box', r.kind]} />
        <text x="64" y={y(i) + 31} text-anchor="middle">{r.label}</text>
        <rect x="236" y={y(i) + 4} width="120" height="44" rx="3" class:list={['box', r.kind]} />
        <text x="296" y={y(i) + 31} text-anchor="middle">{r.label}</text>
        {r.kind === 'ok' && <line x1="126" y1={y(i) + 26} x2="234" y2={y(i) + 26} class="link-ok" />}
        {(r.kind === 'bad' || r.kind === 'unknown') && (
          <>
            <line x1="126" y1={y(i) + 26} x2="168" y2={y(i) + 26} class:list={['link', r.kind]} />
            <line x1="192" y1={y(i) + 26} x2="234" y2={y(i) + 26} class:list={['link', r.kind]} />
            <text x="180" y={y(i) + 31} text-anchor="middle" class="mark">{r.kind === 'bad' ? '✕' : '?'}</text>
          </>
        )}
        <text x="180" y={y(i) + (r.kind ? 16 : 31)} text-anchor="middle" class="note">{r.note}</text>
      </g>
    ))}
  </svg>
  <figcaption>
    Matching channel settings put the signal on the same channel. Receiving a frame also needs compatible
    framing, and reading the message needs the same mesh protocol.
  </figcaption>
  <table>
    <caption>Text version of the diagram</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Layer</th><th scope="col">Meshtastic node vs MeshCore node</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">App</th><td>Different apps, each talking only to its own radio.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Phone–radio link</th><td>Local Bluetooth or USB link on each side; never crosses between the two.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Mesh protocol</th><td><strong>Different.</strong> Each uses its own packet format, so even a received frame can't be read as a native message.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Framing (sync word, header, CRC)</th><td><strong>Not shown to match.</strong> The projects set their sync word in different code, and this course hasn't tested whether one stock radio accepts the other's frames.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Channel settings</th><td>Same frequency, bandwidth, spreading factor and coding rate in this example, so the signal lands on the other radio's channel.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</figure>

<style>
  svg {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 30rem;
    height: auto;
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--__sl-font);
  }
  text {
    fill: var(--sl-color-gray-1);
    font-size: 13px;
  }
  .hdr {
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 12px;
  }
  .note {
    fill: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-style: italic;
  }
  .mark {
    fill: var(--sl-color-gray-1);
    font-size: 16px;
  }
  .box {
    fill: var(--sl-color-gray-6);
    stroke: var(--sl-color-gray-4);
  }
  .box.bad {
    stroke: var(--sl-color-gray-1);
    stroke-dasharray: 5 3;
  }
  .box.unknown {
    stroke: var(--sl-color-gray-2);
    stroke-dasharray: 1 3;
  }
  .link-ok {
    stroke: var(--sl-color-accent);
    stroke-width: 3;
  }
  .link {
    stroke: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
    stroke-width: 2;
    stroke-dasharray: 3 4;
  }
  .link.unknown {
    stroke-dasharray: 1 4;
  }
</style>
